TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"no","where":null},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Whole report"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"RAAN of 27.33° is said to indicate the launch facility location, but Kourou's longitude (~-52.8°) does not match; the value is unjustified."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table lists access 15 as an exact duplicate of access 14. The 15 entries, mean (580.786 s) and total (8711.797 s) therefore include a spurious pass."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Claims the satellite is in constant sunlight on the descending pass. A sun-synchronous orbit still has eclipse seasons, so this is overstated."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Argues high altitude is 'desirable' because it yields low-resolution images, which contradicts the stated need for imaging quality. The swath argument is muddled."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"States uncontrolled descent leads to burn-up, but at 600 km orbital lifetime is decades, not months. Drag-sail and graveyard options are not quantitatively assessed."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag sail sentence is truncated mid-sentence ('utilising a large surface area to ') and cannot be read as written."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Relies on Wikipedia pages as sources ('Timeline of spaceflight', '2021 in spaceflight'), which the brief explicitly excludes."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Falcon 9 'expected lifespan of 100 launches' is asserted without citation. Launch table citations [49]-[74] are absent from the bibliography, and [10] is attached to a different source."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Reference [5] contains a stray 'f.' and reference [10] is garbled ('[10 d.'). Web-only sources are used throughout with no literature."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Figures 1-5 are unnumbered and the tables lack numbers and captions, failing the labelling requirement."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E is unflown, and the 2024 first-launch date is treated as firm despite the mission being scheduled for 2025, which weakens the launch plan."}],"overall":"A clearly structured report with a sound SSO design and a relevant mission rationale. Weaknesses include a wrong RAAN justification, a duplicated access row, an incomplete EOL section, Wikipedia sourcing, unnumbered figures and tables, and missing STK files and appendix. Overall adequate.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale, and single-spacecraft justification are clearly and well argued with supporting detail. Users are only vaguely identified: the operator is left as either a university or government, and the customers are hedged with 'would likely'. Referencing is mostly web links, with no literature sources. The low end of Accomplished is appropriate.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major requirements are addressed with STK screenshots and an access table. However, the RAAN justification is wrong. The access table duplicates access 15 as access 14, which skews the mean. The EOL drag-sail sentence is truncated, and the 600 km re-entry claim is unsupported. Wikipedia is used as a source, no hand calculations or STK files are provided, and Vega E is unbuilt. Support is adequate rather than strong.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"To compare existing spaceflight mission plans, a wiki sorted directory"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the configuration and notes the launch-vehicle risk and fallback. It is largely generic, however: it restates the design without quantitative findings such as pass count or contact statistics. The 'hopefully contribute' closing is unsupported by the evidence presented.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"although it has not been built yet"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Structure is clear, with contents, executive summary, and headed sections. Figures are captioned but unnumbered, tables have no numbers, and there are incomplete sentences and garbled references. Table citations [49]-[74] do not appear in the bibliography, and stray characters and typos appear throughout. Presentation is inconsistent enough to impede reading.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[10 d."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"}]}]}
